  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Good boundary specimen, limited scenario solution: deterministic CBOR plus explicit pCID dispatch lets a feed/CAS layer reject bad chunks locally without central enforcement, but this draft leaves chunk mismatch records, retry policy, and peer-local accounting to payload or wrapper protocols, so corrupt-chunk coverage is only partial.",
    "strengths": [
      "Deterministic CBOR bytes support stable hashing and signing of exact envelopes.",
      "Unknown-pCID opaque store/forward matches sparse-knowledge and no-speculative-parse goals.",
      "The spec clearly leaves CAS and feed semantics to the payload layer instead of hiding them in the envelope."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The specimen does not itself define chunk CID binding, reject records, or retry semantics.",
      "Peer-local accounting is delegated but not described.",
      "Opaque unknown payloads reduce auditability when a later peer lacks the handler."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Unsupported or corrupt content can be relayed as opaque bytes until a validating peer inspects it.",
      "Wrapper-based provenance may diverge across pCIDs and complicate consistent blame or audit trails.",
      "The draft is unfrozen and its pCID/handler ecosystem is not yet durable evidence."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Which payload protocol carries the expected CID, delivered bytes, and mismatch proof?",
      "How are local rejection and accounting records linked to the envelope and any wrapper signatures?",
      "Are hop or authorship proofs wrapper-only, and how are their pCIDs discovered over long time horizons?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
